  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by \n\n$$\nx(t)=16\\sqrt{t}-{t}^{4},\\,t\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the velocity at time $t=1.$\n\n$v(1)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The velocity is the derivative of the position function $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(16\\sqrt{t}-{t}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{2\\sqrt{t}}-4{t}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{t}}-4{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the velocity at $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the velocity function above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{1}}-4({1}^{3}) \\\\  & =8-4 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
